<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Aba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1044">1044</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Aba Sámuel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Aba was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1041 to 1044. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> of the Aba kindred and brother-in-law of Stephen I, he was raised to the throne when the nobles expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-orseolo">Peter Orseolo</a>. His rule provoked opposition among the nobles and the intervention of Emperor Henry III, who defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-menfo">Battle of Ménfő</a> in 1044; he was captured and killed soon afterwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-aba.jpg" alt="Samuel Aba" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Márk Kálti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%A9pes_kr%C3%B3nika_-_48.oldal_-_Aba_S%C3%A1muel_kir%C3%A1ly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Samuel came from the Aba family, which held extensive domains in the region of the Mátra Hills. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a> and later Hungarian chronicles stress its non-Hungarian origin, calling it Cuman or Khwarezmian, and modern historians write that the Abas headed the Kabar tribes, a people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> origin who joined the Hungarians in the 9th century. Gyula Kristó argues that both Samuel's Khazar origin and his first name suggest a family that adhered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. Samuel founded an abbey at Abasár, and Stephen I, the first King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, made him the first palatine of Hungary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Stephen died on 15 August 1038, and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-orseolo">Peter Orseolo</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, who preferred his German and Italian courtiers, set Samuel aside. In 1041 discontented Hungarian nobles expelled Peter and elected Samuel king. The Hungarian chronicles agree that he favoured commoners over nobles, and Hermann of Reichenau called him the tyrant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He abolished the laws of Peter and had many of his supporters killed or tortured. In 1042 he stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, which provoked an invasion by the German king Henry III the following year. Samuel gave up the territories west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leitha">Leitha</a> and Morava and agreed to pay tribute, funded by taxes on the clergy and seizure of Church estates. Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-csanad">Gerard of Csanád</a> refused to perform the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> crown-wearing ceremony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Henry III invaded again in 1044 to restore Peter. The decisive battle was fought at Ménfő near Győr, where Henry routed Samuel's army, the larger of the two. Samuel's fate is uncertain: nearly contemporary German sources say he was soon captured and executed on Peter's order, while 14th-century Hungarian chronicles say he fled up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> and was killed by locals, and was buried in a nearby church and later moved to his family's monastery at Abasár. No report on his widow and children survives, but historians suppose that the later Aba family descended from him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Aba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Füzesabony, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.75,20.41&amp;year=1034" aria-label="Füzesabony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Samuel Aba"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4451 6000h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4451 6000h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.75,20.41&amp;year=1034">What was here in 1034? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
